  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "now sports protein powder review",
      "now sports whey protein isolate",
      "now sports pea protein",
      "now soy protein isolate",
      "now sports egg white protein",
      "now foods protein powder",
      "is now protein third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Isolate Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-isolate/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">NOW Sports Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      NOW Foods' sports line sells plain, mostly unsweetened whey isolate, pea, soy and egg white protein at some of the lowest prices per gram in our database. Here is what is on each label, what the testing claims mean and who should buy which tub.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#whats-in-it">What Is in It?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which One to Buy</a>
      <a href="#vs">NOW vs Naked and Others</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#where">Where to Buy</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          NOW Sports makes some of the cheapest clean-label protein you can buy: its unflavored pea protein costs about $1.02 per 25 g and its whey isolate about $1.72, and the whey isolate is listed on the Informed Sport registry. The catch is that the unflavored tubs taste of very little, the pea is earthy, and stock comes and goes. This review checks four NOW Sports powders label by label, against Naked, Transparent Labs and Gold Standard.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. NOW Sports Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            NOW Sports protein powder is good if you want a plain, cheap, tested protein and do not mind flavoring it yourself. The Whey Protein Isolate is the one to buy for most people: two ingredients, 24 g of protein in 110 calories, no sweetener, a listing on the Informed Sport registry and a cost of about $1.55 to $1.72 per 25 g of protein in the 5 lb tub. The Pea Protein is the budget vegan pick at about $1.02 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy it if you blend shakes with fruit, coffee, cocoa or oats, if you are drug-tested in sport, or if you want to avoid sucralose and stevia. Skip it if you want a powder that tastes like a milkshake with water, because the unflavored tubs taste of almost nothing and the pea tastes earthy. Skip the egg white unless you specifically need a dairy-free animal protein.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">NOW Sports powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein Isolate (unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1.55-1.72</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pea Protein (unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1.02</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soy Protein Isolate (unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1.03</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Egg White Protein (unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2.13</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four NOW Sports Protein Powders Worth Knowing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ranked for most buyers. Label numbers are from the Supplement Facts panels on Vitacost's listings (NOW's own site blocked our checks), for the unflavored versions unless noted; prices are Vitacost list prices on the dates given.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is NOW Sports Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, for the whey isolate: NOW Sports Whey Protein Isolate appears on the Informed Sport registry at <a href="https://sport.wetestyoutrust.com/supplement-search/now-foods/now-sports-whey-protein-isolate" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">sport.wetestyoutrust.com</a>, with unflavored batches listed into 2025 and 2026. Informed Sport tests every batch it certifies for substances banned by the World Anti-Doping Agency before release, which is the standard athletes who face drug testing should look for.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the pea, soy and egg white powders, the Informed Sport mark appears on the retailer listings and NOW Sports is on Informed Sport's list of certified brands, but the registry's bot check blocked our product-level search. Treat those three as brand claims we could not confirm. If you are tested, look up the batch number printed on your tub on the registry before you use it; certification covers listed batches, not the brand as a whole.
          </p>

          <p>
            It helps to know what Informed Sport does and does not cover. It screens for banned substances such as stimulants and anabolic agents. It does not verify that the protein per scoop matches the label, and it does not test for heavy metals. For contents, look for NSF Contents Certified or a brand's own published certificates of analysis. We found no heavy metal test results for these four NOW Sports products in the sources we use, so we cannot say how they compare on lead; plant proteins as a group tend to carry more than whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            NOW Foods itself is a family-owned company founded in Illinois in 1968 that runs its own quality labs, and it has publicized its own lab tests of other brands' supplements bought on Amazon. That background is a reason to expect a careful manufacturer, not a substitute for a certificate. On the label itself, the whey isolate carries kosher dairy and halal marks, and the pea and soy carry Non-GMO Project marks, per the listings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in NOW Sports Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost nothing besides protein, which is the point of the line. The unflavored whey isolate lists whey protein isolate and sunflower lecithin at under 1 percent. The pea lists yellow pea protein isolate alone. The soy lists non-GMO soy protein isolate alone. The egg white lists egg white powder, 1 g of a branched-chain amino acid blend and sunflower lecithin. None of the four unflavored versions has a sweetener, gum, thickener, creamer or flavoring, per the panels on Vitacost's listings at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The whey isolate is the leanest per gram of protein. A 28 g scoop holds 24 g of protein, which is about 86 percent protein by weight, with 1 g of carbohydrate, no fat and 45 mg of sodium. NOW's typical amino profile shows about 2.5 g of leucine per scoop, the amino acid most linked to switching on muscle protein synthesis, which is in line with other whey isolates. Some NOW marketing copy says 25 g of protein; the panel says 24 g, and we use the panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pea protein is 24 g of protein in a 33 g serving, about 73 percent protein, with 2 g of fat, 8 mg of iron and 320 mg of sodium. That sodium figure is high for a single-ingredient powder and comes from how pea protein is extracted, not from added salt. Leucine is about 2 g per serving, lower than whey but respectable for a plant protein. If you are watching sodium, the soy isolate has about half as much per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            The soy isolate is measured by volume: 1/3 cup, or 24 g, for 20 g of protein and 90 calories. The egg white is measured the same way: 1/4 cup, or 20 g, for 15 g of protein and 70 calories. Some retailers show a 16 g, 80 calorie panel for the egg white, which may be a newer formula, so check the tub you receive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do the flavored versions add sweetener?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. NOW sells the whey isolate in Creamy Vanilla and Creamy Chocolate and the pea in Vanilla Toffee and Creamy Chocolate. Flavored versions add flavoring and a sweetener, which changes the label and the protein per gram. We have not checked those labels in our database, so read the panel on the flavored tub if sweetener type matters to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Does NOW Sports Protein Cost per 25 g?
          </h2>

          <p>
            NOW Sports pea and soy cost about $1.02 to $1.03 per 25 g of protein and the whey isolate about $1.55 to $1.72, which puts all three in the cheapest third of the 104 priced powders in our database, where the median is $2.26. The egg white, at about $2.13, is the only NOW protein that costs close to the average.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">NOW Sports powder (size)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vitacost list price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Servings</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey Protein Isolate (5 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$134.01 (Nov 23); $120.28 (Nov 1)</td>
