        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Do Buyers Say About NOW Sports Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly like the whey isolate's taste because it has so little of one, and are split on the pea. We have not tasted these powders; this is a summary of buyer reviews on Vitacost's listings in November 2026, which are a smaller and less filtered sample than Amazon's.
          </p>

          <p>
            The unflavored whey isolate had 247 reviews on Vitacost, with 81 percent at five stars and 3 percent at one star. Reviewers describe it as having no taste at all or tasting clean, and say it dissolves and mixes easily. The complaints are about smell and aftertaste, with one reviewer comparing it to melted plastic, and one buyer who felt misled that the scoop held only 24 g of protein. Several praised the value of the 5 lb size.
          </p>

          <p>
            The unflavored pea had 606 reviews, with 67 percent at five stars and 5 percent at one star, a noticeably weaker spread. Some reviewers call it neutral or tasteless; others call it bitter, earthy or simply bad. Most say it mixes easily, with a few reporting clumping in humid weather. Buyers who liked it tended to use it in smoothies, oatmeal and baking rather than in water, and praised the short ingredient list and the price. One reviewer said they wanted an updated independent heavy metals report before buying again, which is a fair point for any pea protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical advice from those reviews is simple. Do not drink unflavored NOW protein with water and expect a treat. Blend it with milk or a milk alternative, a banana or frozen berries, cocoa powder or cold brew, or stir it into oats or yogurt. Whey isolate dissolves best in cold liquid; stirring it into very hot coffee can make it clump. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein guide</a> has more ways to use plain powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which NOW Sports Protein Powder Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy the Whey Protein Isolate unless you avoid dairy. It has the most protein per calorie of the four, the best amino acid profile for muscle, the registry listing you can check yourself and a price well under most isolates. It is also low enough in lactose that many people who struggle with whey concentrate do fine with it, though it is not lactose-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the Pea Protein if you are vegan or avoid dairy and want the lowest price. It matches the whey on protein per serving (24 g) at about two-thirds of the price per gram, at the cost of more sodium, a little less leucine and a taste many buyers need to mask. Buy the Soy Protein Isolate instead if you want fewer calories and less sodium from a plant protein and are fine with soy; it has the most protein per calorie of NOW's plant options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the Egg White Protein only if you cannot take dairy, do not want a plant protein, and are not allergic to egg. It costs about twice as much per 25 g as the pea and has only 15 g of protein per serving. For most people who just dislike how whey feels, a whey isolate is the cheaper thing to try first; our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">egg white protein guide</a> covers the alternatives if you do need egg.
          </p>

          <p>
            NOW also sells a whey concentrate and a micellar casein under the NOW Sports name. They are not in our label database yet, so we have not scored them here; read the panels before buying and compare their cost per 25 g with the isolate, since the isolate is often only a little more expensive.
          </p>
        </div>

