        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy NOW Sports Protein, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            NOW Sports is a strong fit for four kinds of buyer. Athletes who face drug testing get a whey isolate on the Informed Sport registry for less than most certified wheys. People who avoid artificial sweeteners and stevia get unsweetened tubs with one or two ingredients. Smoothie, oatmeal and baking users get a neutral protein that does not fight other flavors. And budget-minded vegans get one of the cheapest pea proteins sold.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is a poor fit if you want a shake that tastes good with water, since none of the unflavored tubs will, and the flavored versions are not where NOW's value lies. It is also a poor fit if you need guaranteed stock of one product every month, because NOW's large tubs go out of stock at retailers more often than the big supermarket brands. And if you are sensitive to heavy metals in plant protein, look for a pea or blend with published lead results rather than relying on any brand's general reputation.
          </p>

          <p>
            People with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, and anyone with a milk, soy or egg allergy should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement, and should pick the NOW powder that avoids their allergen. This review is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing between NOW and a flavored mass-market whey on price alone, work out what you would add to make NOW taste good. A banana or a spoon of cocoa costs very little, but a daily carton of milk alternative does not, and a $1.44 flavored whey you drink with water can end up cheaper than a $1.55 unflavored isolate you always blend with extras.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Where Can You Buy NOW Sports Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            NOW Sports protein is sold on Amazon, at Vitacost and iHerb, on NOW's own site, and in many natural food stores and supplement shops. The product cards above link to the Amazon listings for each powder. Prices differ by retailer and change often; at the time of writing, Vitacost showed the 5 lb whey isolate as temporarily unavailable, so check two or three sellers before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            When buying on Amazon, check that the seller is Amazon or NOW Foods and that the tub matches the label you expect, since older listings sometimes show an earlier ingredient list. One Canadian listing for the whey isolate shows soy lecithin rather than sunflower lecithin, which matters to people avoiding soy. If you are tested in sport, note the batch number on the tub and confirm it on the Informed Sport registry.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a whey isolate with more flavor choice, start with our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> rankings, where NOW Sports sits among the cheapest certified options. If you want a plant protein, our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">best pea protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-soy-protein-powder/">best soy protein powder</a> guides compare NOW with the other single-source options on label and price.
          </p>
