        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the unflavored whey to taste sweet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NOW's unflavored isolate has no sweetener or flavoring, so with water it tastes of faint milk and little else. Blend it with fruit, cocoa, coffee or a flavored milk, or buy a flavored whey if you only ever drink shakes with water. Buyers who knew this going in rate it highly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every NOW powder is registry-checked</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                We could confirm the whey isolate on the Informed Sport registry. For the pea, soy and egg white the mark appears on listings, but we could not confirm a product-level entry. If you are drug-tested, check your tub's batch number on the registry yourself before using it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing per-scoop protein across NOW tubs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The four powders use different serving sizes: a 28 g scoop of whey, 33 g of pea, a 1/3 cup of soy and a 1/4 cup of egg white. Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not cost per serving, or the egg white looks cheaper than it really is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sodium in the pea protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The unflavored pea has 320 mg of sodium per serving, from processing rather than added salt. Two shakes a day add 640 mg. That is fine for most people but worth knowing on a low-sodium diet, where the soy isolate (170 mg) or whey isolate (45 mg) fits better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the egg white for value</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Egg white is the most expensive NOW protein per gram at about $2.13 per 25 g, with only 15 g per serving. Buy it if you need a dairy-free animal protein. If you only want something cheaper or gentler than whey concentrate, the whey isolate or pea is better value.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is NOW Sports protein powder third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The whey isolate is: NOW Sports Whey Protein Isolate appears on the Informed Sport registry, which tests listed batches for banned substances, with unflavored batches recorded into 2025 and 2026. The pea, soy and egg white listings show the Informed Sport mark, but we could not confirm product-level entries. Informed Sport does not test for heavy metals or verify protein content.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is NOW protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The unflavored NOW Sports powders are about as clean as protein powder gets: whey isolate with under 1 percent sunflower lecithin, pea or soy protein isolate alone, or egg white with added BCAAs. None has a sweetener, gum or flavoring. We found no published heavy metal results for these products, which is the one gap in an otherwise simple label.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does NOW Sports whey isolate contain lactose or artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It contains no sweetener of any kind in the unflavored version, per the label. As a whey isolate it is low in lactose, with 1 g of total carbohydrate per scoop, but NOW does not label it lactose-free, so people with severe lactose intolerance should be cautious. The flavored versions add sweetener and flavoring; check their labels.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in NOW Sports whey isolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  24 g of protein per 28 g scoop, per the Supplement Facts panel, with 110 calories, 1 g of carbohydrate and no fat or sugar. Some NOW marketing copy says 25 g. We use the panel figure. That works out to about 86 percent protein by weight, typical of a good whey isolate, with about 2.5 g of leucine.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which NOW Sports protein flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most buyers choose unflavored, and reviewers describe the whey isolate as clean or tasteless and the pea as neutral to earthy or bitter. The flavored whey isolate comes in Creamy Vanilla and Creamy Chocolate and the pea in Vanilla Toffee and Creamy Chocolate. We have not tasted them, and our database does not yet hold their labels.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the most common NOW Sports protein complaints?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  In Vitacost reviews the whey isolate's main complaints were a smell or aftertaste some buyers disliked and the 24 g serving when some copy says 25 g. The pea drew more complaints, mostly about a bitter or earthy taste and occasional clumping in humid conditions. Stock gaps on the 5 lb tubs are another common frustration.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is NOW Sports pea protein good for building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, when you eat enough of it. A serving gives 24 g of protein and about 2 g of leucine, less leucine than whey but enough for most people when part of a high-protein diet. Pea protein lacks a little methionine, so mixing protein sources across the day helps. At about $1.02 per 25 g it is one of the cheapest ways to add plant protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

